I [wrote recently](/posts/20260330-context-was-always-the-job/) about how context was always the job — that the value of engineering was never in the syntax, it was in holding enough context to be precise about the right things. The more I've sat with that idea, the more I keep noticing it in other fields too.

The right course of action always depends on what you're trying to do. Build a car, and you need to know whether it should be fast or fuel-efficient or cheap. Write a news article, and you need to know who it's for and what they already understand. Give an AI agent a task, and you need to give it enough context to know what "good" looks like in your situation. This is obvious. But we keep acting as if the right answer is always the right answer — as if solutions are universal and context is a detail you fill in later.

My career has moved through journalism, content strategy, information architecture, design systems, and software engineering. I now spend most of my time on AI integration. These are the fields I can see from where I sit, and from here I've noticed something: software engineering, content strategy, and AI context engineering have all landed on structurally analogous problems around how shared understanding crosses boundaries. Different problems in detail, but with a striking family resemblance. Underneath all three sits a deeper insight from linguistic anthropology about why context has to be actively built in the first place. Each tradition spent years working through the hard parts. They haven't been leveraging each other's lessons nearly enough.

> **tl;dr**
>
> - Software engineering (DDD), content strategy, and AI context engineering have landed on structurally analogous problems around shared understanding — with a deeper theoretical foundation in linguistic anthropology
> - They share intellectual ancestry but developed their solutions largely in parallel, and haven't been leveraging each other's lessons nearly enough
> - AI is making the resemblance visible because context failures with LLMs are instant — though often dangerously subtle
> - If you're treating context as a domain-specific concern, you're missing transferable solutions from adjacent fields

## The theoretical layer

Before the engineering traditions, a deeper observation. Alessandro Duranti and Charles Goodwin published [*Rethinking Context*](https://web.archive.org/web/20030312200748/http://www.sscnet.ucla.edu/anthro/faculty/duranti/reprints/rethco.pdf) in 1992, which changed how linguists think about context. Their argument: context isn't static background to communication. It's actively produced through interaction. People don't just operate within a context; they build it together as they talk, gesture, and respond.

<aside class="kh-marginnote">Paul Dourish <a href="https://www.dourish.com/publications/2004/PUC2004-context.pdf">translated this thesis for computing in 2004</a>, arguing context is an "interactional problem" not a "representational problem." But the work landed in human-computer interaction (HCI), not in DDD, content strategy, or AI. Even when a bridge gets built, it stays within its own field.</aside>

For decades this was a philosophical observation about how meaning works between humans — interesting but not directly applicable to engineering. Large language models changed that. LLMs are the first engineering artifact where meaning is produced through interaction in something close to the way Duranti and Goodwin described. When you prompt an agent, you're co-constructing context through dialogue: what you say shapes what the model treats as relevant, which shapes your next input, which reshapes the model's understanding. The context isn't static input; it's built turn by turn, exactly as in human conversation.

That makes their observation newly practical. If context is always produced through interaction, not inherited from some fixed background, then every system that crosses a boundary between different groups, platforms, or agents needs a deliberate mechanism to produce it. The engineering traditions below are the *how*. Duranti and Goodwin describe the *why* — and LLMs are why it matters to engineering now in a way it didn't before.

## The engineering threads

**Software engineering (2003).** Eric Evans published [*Domain-Driven Design*](https://www.domainlanguage.com/ddd/), arguing that the hard problem of software is shared understanding of the domain, not the code. His prescription: build a "ubiquitous language" that domain experts and developers both use. Formalise it. Draw explicit boundaries ("bounded contexts") around different models so they don't corrupt each other. The primary artifact of engineering, in Evans' framing, is the shared model, not the running system. I didn't encounter Evans until years after I'd been doing content modelling, and the overlap is striking.

**Content strategy (2009--2012).** Daniel Jacobson at NPR built [COPE](https://www.programmableweb.com/news/cope-create-once-publish-everywhere/2009/10/13) (Create Once, Publish Everywhere): content modelled as structured data with standardised interfaces, not as presentation-specific blobs. Karen McGrane's [*Content Strategy for Mobile*](https://abookapart.com/products/content-strategy-for-mobile) sharpened the point: content that's trapped in a specific visual format ("blobs") can't travel across channels. You have to break it into meaningful "chunks" named by what they are, not how they look. "News item" survives a redesign; "featured card with sidebar layout" doesn't.

<aside class="kh-marginnote">McGrane's "blobs vs. chunks" and Evans' entities and value objects aren't the same problem — one is about content portability, the other about identity and lifecycle in domain models. But the structural move is the same: decompose opaque bundles into meaningful, named units. Content strategists could have borrowed Evans' formalisms; DDD practitioners could have learned from McGrane's portability patterns.</aside>

**AI context engineering (2025--2026).** The current wave. An LLM has general knowledge of the universe but not your project's constraints, your team's vocabulary, or why the module is shaped the way it is. The bottleneck isn't the model; it's [the context you provide](/posts/20251003-effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents/). Practitioners are building CLAUDE.md files, project briefs, scoped rules, and [structured documentation for AI consumption](/posts/20250929-ai-must-rtfm-context-curators/). The discipline is new. The problem it's solving is not.

I should be honest about the limits of this framing. These are the traditions I can see from where I sit — they're shaped by my career, not by a natural taxonomy. Knowledge management (Nonaka & Takeuchi's tacit-to-explicit conversion, 1995), library science, systems thinking, and organisational theory all have deep roots in similar territory. And the traditions I am covering aren't fully independent: content strategy grew out of computing and information architecture, AI context engineering is practiced almost entirely by software engineers, and DDD drew on earlier modelling traditions. They share intellectual ancestry even where they developed solutions separately.

There's also a fair counterargument: maybe these traditions haven't cross-pollinated because the problems are different enough in practice that the parallels don't cash out. A DDD practitioner reading McGrane might find it interesting but not actionable. I don't think that's the full story — but I should show rather than assert.

## What they share

The specific problems are different: DDD is about modelling business domains to reduce software complexity, content strategy is about portability across channels, linguistic anthropology is about how meaning emerges in interaction, and AI context engineering is about feeding the right information into a stateless model. But the structural patterns that emerged are strikingly similar:

**Context is produced, not inherited.** This is where Duranti and Goodwin started, and every tradition landed somewhere close. Evans' domain modelling is collaborative and iterative. McGrane's content schemas don't exist until someone defines them. AI context windows don't fill themselves. In every case, context is something you build on purpose or it doesn't exist.

**Context must be formalised to cross a boundary.** Evans' bounded contexts need explicit translation layers. McGrane's structured content needs schemas to travel between platforms. AI agents need structured context documents to work across sessions and tasks. Informal, tacit understanding works within a team. It breaks the moment you need to share across one. And the failure mode scales with speed: in software, what I've been calling [comprehension debt](/posts/20260328-digesting-comprehension-debt/) accumulates silently; in content, [syndication breaks](/posts/20260327-content-architecture-shipping-container/) compound slowly; in AI, hallucination is instant.

## The weaving has started, but it hasn't gone mainstream

<aside class="kh-marginnote">At <a href="https://www.infoq.com/news/2024/03/Evans-ddd-experiment-llm/">Explore DDD 2024</a>, Evans explored the analogy that a fine-tuned language model is essentially a bounded context. The DDD community is aware of AI, but mostly asking "how can AI help us do DDD?" rather than the reverse.</aside>

Different conferences, different journals, different vocabularies. Linguistic anthropologists don't attend DDD Europe. Content strategists don't read *Patterns of Enterprise Application Architecture*. AI engineers building agent architectures aren't, for the most part, reading Karen McGrane.

Which is ironic, given that most of these fields argue for shared language as a core practice.

Birgitta Bockeler's [context engineering article](https://martinfowler.com/articles/exploring-gen-ai/context-engineering-coding-agents.html) on martinfowler.com is the most widely-read piece in this space right now. It describes practices that echo DDD: scoped rules tied to file types, project-specific conventions, skills as lazy-loaded context modules. It doesn't reference DDD, but that's a scope decision, not an oversight — it's a practical guide to coding agent context, not a theoretical treatise. The interesting question isn't why Bockeler didn't cite Evans. It's whether twenty years of lessons about formalising shared understanding in DDD could make practices like hers more systematic.

Meanwhile, Rod Johnson (creator of the Spring Framework) has been [arguing](https://medium.com/@springrod/context-engineering-needs-domain-understanding-b4387e8e4bf8) that context engineering is incomplete without domain modelling. His DICE framework argues that domain models should structure how context gets selected and organised, rather than treating the context window as an ever-growing string. A small but growing group is making this connection — Evans himself, [James Croft](https://www.jamescroft.co.uk/applying-domain-driven-design-principles-to-multi-agent-ai-systems/) at Microsoft, a handful of academic papers — but it hasn't penetrated the mainstream AI tooling ecosystem yet. And almost none of these voices are looking further afield to content strategy or linguistics.

## The AI moment as forcing function

Before AI, context failures were slow. A misaligned contractor takes weeks to deliver the wrong thing. A content schema that doesn't match across properties causes a slow bleed of broken syndication that nobody notices until it compounds. Comprehension debt accumulates silently while metrics stay green.

AI made the failure instant — but not necessarily obvious. A missing bounded context in software produces a runtime error or visibly wrong behaviour. A missing bounded context in AI produces a plausible-sounding wrong answer. Hallucination looks like correct output. That's what makes it dangerous, and it's why the speed argument is more complicated than "bad in, bad out." The feedback loop is faster, yes, but the failures are subtler, and the cost of catching them late is higher because they look like they worked.

What the speed does is compress the cycle. You hit the consequences of missing context in seconds rather than weeks, so the pattern becomes harder to ignore even if the individual failures are harder to spot. That compression is why the discourse is converging now. When context failures were slow, each tradition could afford to treat them as a domain-specific problem. When they're fast, the structural resemblance becomes harder to miss, and the solutions start to look transferable.

## What a transferred lesson looks like

The post would be incomplete if it argued for weaving without actually weaving. Here's a non-trivial example from my own work.

At UNDRR, we syndicate content from Drupal across multiple downstream platforms through API feeds. Over the years, a recurring problem: a developer misspells a field name in the CMS, say `summray` instead of `summary`. A downstream JavaScript platform consumes that API and builds around the typo. Months later, someone fixes the typo in Drupal. Now the downstream platform breaks, because it was built against the misspelled field. So we add a translation layer to preserve the old typo alongside the fix. This happens again. And again. Each time, the downstream system absorbs more of the upstream system's historical mistakes, and the mapping between them gets more fragile.

DDD has a name for the solution: the [anticorruption layer](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/anti-corruption-layer). It's a deliberate translation boundary between two systems that prevents one system's model from leaking into another's. Instead of each downstream consumer adapting to whatever the upstream happens to expose (typos and all), you build an explicit layer that translates between the external model and your clean internal model. The upstream can change; your internal model stays coherent.

I didn't know this pattern had a name until I started reading Evans. I'd been solving the same problem with ad hoc translation scripts and field-mapping spreadsheets for years. Knowing the pattern wouldn't have just saved time; it would have changed how I structured the integration from the start. An anticorruption layer is a design decision you make at the boundary, not a patch you apply after the damage compounds. Content architecture teams hit this problem constantly and solve it with duct tape because the formalised solution lives in a tradition they've never read.

The same pattern applies directly to AI context engineering. When an agent consumes data from multiple systems with different vocabularies, you need an explicit translation layer, not a prompt that says "figure it out." The anticorruption layer pattern gives you a name for this, a place to put it in your architecture, and twenty years of lessons about what goes wrong when you skip it.

That's one transferred lesson. I suspect there are dozens. But they won't transfer unless people in these fields start reading across the aisle.

## From where I sit

I'm not arguing these traditions should merge. I'm not even sure a unified theory would be useful. What I am doing is noting that the threads are visible from where I sit, and they haven't been woven together.

My recent posts on [context as infrastructure](/posts/20260330-context-was-always-the-job/), [content architecture](/posts/20260327-content-architecture-shipping-container/), and [comprehension debt](/posts/20260328-digesting-comprehension-debt/) each draw on one or two of these traditions. What I hadn't done until now is step back and notice the convergence itself.

If you're working on context engineering for AI and you haven't read Evans on ubiquitous language, there are twenty years of lessons on formalising shared understanding that you're rediscovering from scratch. If you're doing DDD and haven't looked at how content strategists model structured content across platforms, you're missing a parallel tradition that solved the "how does this travel across boundaries" problem for editorial organisations.

And underneath all of it, Duranti and Goodwin is worth your time. Not as another engineering method, but as the theoretical foundation for why the engineering methods keep converging: context has to be produced, or it doesn't exist.

The way I've come to see it: context is a verb, not a noun. It's something you actively build so that the pieces — people, systems, content, agents — can work together. None of these traditions own that insight. All of them arrived at it. The weaving is starting — but it hasn't reached mainstream practice in any of these fields yet. There's plenty left to pull together.

